    "created_at": "2025-11-28T04:38:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-11-28T04:38:17Z",
    "body": "> I guess we should have a follow-up issue on actually using realm to allocate the various global objects. I think Web IDL does allow for it now.\r\n\r\nI'm wondering if there's a problem there. I gather that (e.g.) \"a new <code>Window</code> object\" is a shorthand for \"a new object implementing the interface <code>Window</code>\", which is a reference to [this Web IDL algorithm](https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#new), which then invokes the [internally create a new object implementing the interface](https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#internally-create-a-new-object-implementing-the-interface) algorithm, which makes several references to its _realm_ parameter, and calls other algorithms that also use _realm_. The thing is, in this scenario, that _realm_ isn't complete, so it's possible that some of those uses of _realm_ are ill-defined. E.g., step 1 invokes the [exposed](https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#dfn-exposed) algorithm, which refers to _realm_.[[GlobalObject]], which hasn't been set yet.\r\n",
